1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,634
A sunflower stem.

2
00:00:01,634 --> 00:00:02,680
A maize stalk.

3
00:00:02,680 --> 00:00:05,577
The tough threads left
after you chew sugarcane.

4
00:00:05,977 --> 00:00:07,397
All three are stems.

5
00:00:07,397 --> 00:00:11,771
Cut them across, and the pipes inside
are laid out in two very different

6
00:00:11,771 --> 00:00:12,671
ways.

7
00:00:12,755 --> 00:00:16,736
Aarav stopped at a sugarcane juice
cart after his ride and asked: why are

8
00:00:16,736 --> 00:00:19,378
those hard threads spread
all through the cane?

9
00:00:19,728 --> 00:00:21,789
Because sugarcane is a monocot.

10
00:00:21,789 --> 00:00:24,047
Its vascular bundles are scattered.

11
00:00:24,047 --> 00:00:27,532
A sunflower, a dicot, keeps
its bundles in a ring.

12
00:00:28,032 --> 00:00:32,295
By the end, you will read both stem
sections layer by layer, and see how

13
00:00:32,295 --> 00:00:34,197
the xylem in a stem is built.

14
00:00:34,847 --> 00:00:36,371
First, the big picture.

15
00:00:36,721 --> 00:00:41,316
A dicot stem, like sunflower: bundles
arranged in a ring, with a pith at

16
00:00:41,316 --> 00:00:42,216
the centre.

17
00:00:42,648 --> 00:00:47,130
A monocot stem, like maize: many
bundles scattered through a large ground

18
00:00:47,130 --> 00:00:48,030
tissue.

19
00:00:48,220 --> 00:00:51,866
Watch for four clues: the hypodermis,
the bundles, the cambium and the

20
00:00:51,866 --> 00:00:52,766
pith.

21
00:00:52,963 --> 00:00:56,658
Let's zoom into a young
sunflower stem, a dicot.

22
00:00:57,008 --> 00:00:59,702
Outside is the epidermis,
with a thin cuticle.

23
00:00:59,702 --> 00:01:02,052
It may bear trichomes and a few stomata.

24
00:01:02,552 --> 00:01:06,588
Below it, the hypodermis: a few layers
of collenchyma that give the young

25
00:01:06,588 --> 00:01:07,510
stem strength.

26
00:01:07,510 --> 00:01:10,640
Then rounded parenchyma cells,
with spaces between them.

27
00:01:11,140 --> 00:01:14,314
The innermost layer of the
cortex is the endodermis.

28
00:01:14,314 --> 00:01:18,752
Its cells are rich in starch grains,
so it is called the starch sheath.

29
00:01:19,252 --> 00:01:23,722
Above each bundle sits a half-moon
patch of sclerenchyma: the pericycle.

30
00:01:23,722 --> 00:01:26,231
Between the bundles run medullary rays.

31
00:01:26,231 --> 00:01:28,456
And at the centre lies the pith.

32
00:01:28,956 --> 00:01:33,985
Each bundle is conjoint and open:
phloem outside, cambium in the middle,

33
00:01:33,985 --> 00:01:35,084
xylem inside.

34
00:01:35,734 --> 00:01:39,159
Now watch the xylem in one
stem bundle being built.

35
00:01:39,509 --> 00:01:43,815
The first xylem, the protoxylem, forms
on the inner side, towards the pith.

36
00:01:44,565 --> 00:01:47,437
The later xylem, the metaxylem,
forms outward, towards the edge.

37
00:01:47,437 --> 00:01:49,794
Protoxylem inside, metaxylem
outside: this is endarch.

38
00:01:50,544 --> 00:01:55,035
Phloem sits on the outer side, and the
cambium between them keeps the bundle

39
00:01:55,035 --> 00:01:55,935
open.

40
00:01:56,310 --> 00:01:57,662
Now a maize bundle.

41
00:01:57,662 --> 00:01:59,831
A sheath of sclerenchyma wraps it.

42
00:01:59,831 --> 00:02:02,347
There is no cambium, so it is closed.

43
00:02:03,097 --> 00:02:04,958
Phloem parenchyma is absent.

44
00:02:04,958 --> 00:02:07,943
And inside the bundle,
a cavity holds water.

45
00:02:08,693 --> 00:02:12,950
Now the pair to tell apart: the
dicot stem and the monocot stem.

46
00:02:13,300 --> 00:02:16,213
Both have an epidermis
and vascular bundles.

47
00:02:16,213 --> 00:02:18,659
In both, the bundles are conjoint.

48
00:02:19,109 --> 00:02:23,434
But a dicot stem has a collenchyma
hypodermis and open bundles in a ring.

49
00:02:23,434 --> 00:02:27,777
A monocot stem has a sclerenchyma
hypodermis and closed bundles, scattered.

50
00:02:28,427 --> 00:02:29,327
Your turn.

51
00:02:29,257 --> 00:02:33,456
Bundles scattered, each with a
sclerenchyma sheath, and no cambium.

52
00:02:33,456 --> 00:02:34,917
Sunflower, or maize?

53
00:02:34,917 --> 00:02:36,138
Pause and decide.

54
00:02:39,338 --> 00:02:40,238
Maize!

55
00:02:39,938 --> 00:02:44,419
Scattered, closed bundles with bundle
sheaths belong to a monocot stem.

56
00:02:45,069 --> 00:02:46,183
Remember this.

57
00:02:46,183 --> 00:02:48,805
Dicot stem: open bundles in a ring.

58
00:02:48,805 --> 00:02:51,667
Monocot stem: closed bundles, scattered.

59
00:02:52,267 --> 00:02:54,043
Now, a worked example.

60
00:02:54,043 --> 00:02:57,722
Let's sort four clues into
dicot stem or monocot stem.

61
00:02:58,072 --> 00:03:01,129
An endodermis full of
starch, the starch sheath.

62
00:03:01,129 --> 00:03:02,643
That is a dicot stem.

63
00:03:03,093 --> 00:03:05,850
A sclerenchyma sheath around every bundle.

64
00:03:05,850 --> 00:03:07,580
That is a monocot stem.

65
00:03:08,030 --> 00:03:10,420
Medullary rays between the bundles.

66
00:03:10,420 --> 00:03:12,083
That is a dicot stem.

67
00:03:12,533 --> 00:03:15,140
Water-containing cavities
inside the bundles.

68
00:03:15,140 --> 00:03:16,752
That is a monocot stem.

69
00:03:17,402 --> 00:03:18,864
Now, the exam trap.

70
00:03:19,214 --> 00:03:22,750
Many students think stem xylem
is exarch, just like the root.

71
00:03:23,150 --> 00:03:25,941
But in a stem, the
protoxylem faces the pith.

72
00:03:25,941 --> 00:03:27,354
Stem xylem is endarch.

73
00:03:27,354 --> 00:03:28,723
Root xylem is exarch.

74
00:03:29,373 --> 00:03:30,692
Where is this asked?

75
00:03:30,692 --> 00:03:34,730
As match the following: hypodermis,
starch sheath and bundle types, and

76
00:03:34,730 --> 00:03:36,084
endarch versus exarch.

77
00:03:36,684 --> 00:03:37,856
Exam level now.

78
00:03:37,856 --> 00:03:40,101
Match column one with column two.

79
00:03:40,101 --> 00:03:41,323
Pause and solve.

80
00:03:46,723 --> 00:03:48,129
The answer is B.

81
00:03:48,629 --> 00:03:53,511
Collenchyma lies below the epidermis,
the starch sheath is the endodermis,

82
00:03:53,511 --> 00:03:56,709
and the half-moon
sclerenchyma is the pericycle.

83
00:03:57,209 --> 00:04:00,303
A sclerenchyma bundle sheath
belongs to the monocot stem.

84
00:04:00,903 --> 00:04:02,444
Here is your recap.

85
00:04:02,444 --> 00:04:04,028
Screenshot this card.

86
00:04:07,228 --> 00:04:09,413
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

87
00:04:09,813 --> 00:04:10,713
One.

88
00:04:10,265 --> 00:04:13,917
Why is the endodermis of a dicot
stem called the starch sheath?

89
00:04:14,817 --> 00:04:15,717
Two.

90
00:04:15,152 --> 00:04:16,231
What does endarch mean?

91
00:04:17,131 --> 00:04:18,031
Three.

92
00:04:17,669 --> 00:04:20,213
Name two features of
a monocot stem bundle.

93
00:04:21,313 --> 00:04:25,671
Next time: why is a mango leaf darker
on top, while a grass blade looks

94
00:04:25,671 --> 00:04:27,247
the same on both sides?

95
00:04:27,247 --> 00:04:30,335
We find out in dorsiventral
and isobilateral leaves.
